# base_unittests tests that do not pass on Jam OS yet (tools/cr-test.sh base,
# tools/cr/base-run.py). A "[layer] reason" line, then the full names of the
# tests it explains, one a line (a name may use * as the wildcard). Or one
# line "<pattern> <layer> <reason>". The tests are not run (CR_BASE_ALL=1
# runs them anyway and reports a failure as known), so a `base` PASS means
# every other test passed.
#
# Made 2026-10-16 from CR_BASE_ALL=1 runs of the shards of the suites on
# base_unittests relinked against main's libc (tools/cr/base-run.py: each
# suite a program of its own; tests that crashed alone). The layer is the
# track that owns the fix: posix (libc and its call layer), proc (/proc
# files), fs (a file system under /data: extfs, or the mapping calls),
# launch (starting programs: CB5's launcher patch), jit (executable memory
# by capability), toolchain (the link), musl (musl's own behaviour: the
# same on Linux with musl). A cause marked "?" is a guess from the failing call.
# Findings with their evidence: docs/CR-PLAN.md, "CB2 as built" and "CB2b on ext".
#
# The list is for an ext /data (CB2b, 2026-10-16: the 44 file-system tests that
# only a FAT /data fails are in base_unittests.fat.skip, which base-run.py adds
# when the stick's /data is FAT).
#
# Shrunk 2026-10-17 (CB-RM) from CR_BASE_ALL=1 CR_BASE_LISTED=1 runs of base_unittests
# relinked against main 0fa8eea5 (fork, EFAULT, CBF2a/2b/4, ext limits): what
# passed came off, 596 listed became 259 (docs/CR-PLAN.md, "CB-RM"). The run's
# death tests used gtest's fast style then.
#
# Shrunk again 2026-10-17 (CCS) from CR_BASE_ALL=1 CR_BASE_LISTED=1 runs in base's
# own threadsafe death test style (base-run.py's default again): the clone
# child's calls run on a stack of the layer's own, base_unittests asks for
# `svc exec` (it starts itself and test_child_process, programs on /data), FL3's
# exec, and a forwarded signal that allocates nothing. 38 launching tests and the
# four that set the threadsafe style came off; what is left of the two groups
# went into the groups below by cause (docs/CR-PLAN.md, "CCS").

[posix] the layer's calls allocate from the program's own malloc (fd.c's calloc of a description at every open, procfs's buffers, ...), which Linux's calls never touch. StackDumpSignalHandlerIsMallocFree puts in a malloc that crashes, then raises SIGSEGV: base's handler is async-signal-safe, but its BeingDebugged() opens /proc/self/status, the open's calloc crashes, and the handler prints nothing (either death style). Needs the layer's own heap (docs/CR-PLAN.md, "CCS")
StackTraceDeathTest.StackDumpSignalHandlerIsMallocFree

[posix] inotify_init and inotify_init1 (syscalls 253 and 294) are ENOSYS: no file watching
FilePathWatcher*

[launch] a program on /data started through init's /svc/exec (a forked child's execve of base_unittests: spawn, wait, exit) gets the hand-over init writes it (CR2F, CR-REVIEW-POSIX Q1 decided A): its directory is /, not its caller's, and it is AT_SECURE, so the crt drops TMPDIR from its environment. CurrentDirectory's child finds itself in / ("/tmp vs. /"); the signal-file tests' child looks for its file (a name relative to the directory) in / and never finds it, so it runs to the test's timeout (KillSlowChild; GetTerminationStatus*: still running). Q1's option C (the directory given as a channel) would mend it (docs/CR-PLAN.md, "CCS")
ProcessUtilTest.CurrentDirectory
ProcessUtilTest.GetTerminationStatusCrash
ProcessUtilTest.GetTerminationStatusSigKill
ProcessUtilTest.GetTerminationStatusSigTerm
ProcessUtilTest.KillSlowChild

[posix] a thread's id is never its process's pid here, the first thread's included (a decision: docs/CR-PLAN.md, "CCS"). base's tid cache takes the thread whose tid is the pid for the main thread and refreshes only that one after a fork, so a forked child's main thread keeps its parent's tid (PlatformThreadTidCacheTest); ForkWithFlags' child checks that getpid() is the tid CLONE_CHILD_SETTID stored
ForkWithFlagsTest.UpdatesPidCache
PlatformThreadTidCacheTest.MainThreadFirst
PlatformThreadTidCacheTest.MainThreadSecond

[posix] fcntl's record locks are each process's own: no keeper holds them across processes (libc-test's fcntl too), so the child's lock is granted while the parent holds the file's
FileLockingTest.LockAndUnlockExclusive
FileLockingTest.LockAndUnlockShared
FileLockingTest.UnlockOnCloseExclusive
FileLockingTest.UnlockOnCloseShared
FileLockingTest.UnlockOnExitExclusive
FileLockingTest.UnlockOnExitShared
FileLockingTest.UnlockOnTerminate

[posix] clone of a process sharing our directory and umask (CLONE_FS without CLONE_VM) is refused, EINVAL ("clone flags 529" in the ENOSYS log): LaunchProcess's case with clone_flags CLONE_FS fails, its others pass. Copying instead of sharing would pass it (the child execs at once); not decided (docs/CR-PLAN.md, "CCS")
ProcessUtilTest.LaunchProcess

[posix] a handler run at a call's boundary (a raise: abort(), a kill of ourselves) gets zeros for the registers in its ucontext (signal.c's run_handler), where Linux gives those of the call: the CHECK's abort() handler reads REG_RIP 0 and sends it as the crash address
LoggingTest.CheckCausesDistinctBreakpoints

[proc] another process's /proc/<pid>/fd and /proc/<pid>/stat are ENOSYS (the ENOSYS log: "path /proc/38/fd", "path /proc/30/stat"). MeasureChildCpuUsage would fail even with them: its busy child is a forked child's execve of base_unittests, so the pid it watches waits while the program runs as another (spawn, wait, exit)
ProcessMetricsTest.GetChildOpenFdCount
SystemMetricsTest.MeasureChildCpuUsage

[posix] mincore (CBF2b) answers from the kernel's count of present pages in each mapping: exact for a mapping with all or none of its pages present, a guess between (the first resident pages of the mapping), so a test that asks about one page of a part-resident mapping fails (CheckPageInCore says false for a page that is there; the heap profiler's resident_total reads 0). 12 of the 21 pass (the other Purge* tests, ProcessMemoryDumpTest.CountResidentBytes*). Needs the per-page kernel query: CR-PLAN, "CBF2b notes", decision 1
AlternateTestParams/PartitionAllocTest.PurgeDiscardableNonPageSizedAlloc/0
AlternateTestParams/PartitionAllocTest.PurgeDiscardableNonPageSizedAlloc/1
AlternateTestParams/PartitionAllocTest.PurgeDiscardableNonPageSizedAllocOnSlotBoundary/0
AlternateTestParams/PartitionAllocTest.PurgeDiscardableNonPageSizedAllocOnSlotBoundary/1
AlternateTestParams/PartitionAllocTest.PurgeDiscardableSecondPage/0
AlternateTestParams/PartitionAllocTest.PurgeDiscardableSecondPage/1
AlternateTestParams/PartitionAllocTest.PurgeDiscardableWithFreeListStraightening/0
AlternateTestParams/PartitionAllocTest.PurgeDiscardableWithFreeListStraightening/1
SamplingHeapProfilerTest.GetSamplesWithResidency

[proc] ModuleCacheTest.CheckAgainstProcMaps wants two modules (this one and ld-linux.so) and a static program has one: by design. The other /proc tests pass (CBF4; smaps_rollup CBF6)
ModuleCacheTest.CheckAgainstProcMaps

[posix] dlopen() of a library fails (a static program: musl's stub, logged as `dlopen <library>`)
ElfReaderTestWithCurrentImage.ReadElfBuildId
ImmediateCrashTest.ExpectedOpcodeSequence
NativeLibraryTest.LoadLibrary

[jit] mprotect/mmap of anonymous memory with PROT_EXEC is refused without the JIT right (W^X by design, docs/CR-PLAN.md section 12); the null buffer then faults (DecommitAndZero and InaccessiblePages pass)
PartitionAllocPageAllocatorTest.AllocInaccessibleWillJitLater
PartitionAllocPageAllocatorTest.AllocReadWriteExecute
PartitionAllocPageAllocatorTest.ReadExecutePages

[posix] RecvPidWithMaxDescriptors sends the socket over itself 16 times; the kernel's cycle rule refuses a channel end into its own channel (ETOOMANYREFS, CU1), where Linux keeps it in flight (CBF2a: RecvPid and RecvPidDisconnectedSocket pass)
UnixDomainSocketTest.RecvPidWithMaxDescriptors

[posix] mkfifo/mknod answer EPERM (lx_mknodat, meta.c: the layer has no named pipes and fs.idl no mknod; a symbolic link and every other call these tests make work on ext)
FileUtilTest.CopyDirectoryExclFileOverFifo
FileUtilTest.CopyDirectoryWithNonRegularFiles
FileUtilTest.ReadFileToStringWithNamedPipe
FileUtilTest.TestNonBlockingFileReadLinux

[posix] not a 256-byte limit (CBF6): the test builds 26 levels of 255-byte names through directory descriptors (6.6 KB deep) and a directory description here is its path as text, so mkdirat at the 16th level is ENAMETOOLONG (the layer's PATH_BYTES 4096). Needs directory handles: a design, being planned as docs/DIRHANDLE-PLAN.md
FileUtilTest.DeleteDeep

[musl] fdopen(-1, "w") returns a FILE (musl does not check the descriptor), and the test expects null: the same on Alpine, a test to leave out on any musl build
FileUtilTest.FileToFILE

[fs] a writable shared mapping of a /data file (MemoryMappedFile READ_WRITE, extend, truncate) fails on ext too (WE1: extfs's write-back is not built); the null pointer or bad object the test then uses faults
FilePersistentMemoryAllocatorTest.AcceptableTest
FilePersistentMemoryAllocatorTest.ExtendTest
MemoryMappedFileTest.ExtendableFile
MemoryMappedFileTest.WriteableFile
PersistentHistogramAllocatorTest.CreateSpareFile
PersistentHistogramAllocatorTest.CreateWithFile
PersistentHistogramAllocatorTest.MovePersistentFile
PersistentMemoryAllocatorTest.TruncateTest

[toolchain] PartitionAlloc reserves [64 KiB, 4,097 MiB) (a PROT_NONE zero segment, USER_SPACE_ZERO_SEGMENT_SIZE_MB) by its address as one mapping, and the program's image sits at 0x400000 in the way, so it keeps the 64 KiB it assumes. MH made the hint taken and placement top down (nothing else low), but the programs stay at 0x400000 until they are static PIEs: linked at 1 TiB, headless_shell's calls to BoringSSL's undefined weak functions can't reach 0 (CR-PLAN, "MH"). ZeroSegmentInaccessibleEnd reads the segment's last byte and expects to die: nothing is reserved there ("failed to die"; the Begin test passes; End passed with base_unittests linked at 4 GiB)
PartitionAddressSpaceTest.ZeroSegment
PartitionAddressSpaceTest.ZeroSegmentInaccessibleEnd
